This code solves "House Robber III" on a binary tree using DFS with post-order traversal. For each node, it returns an array [skip, rob], where skip is the maximum money obtainable from the subtree if the current node is not robbed (so we can take the best from children, skipping or robbing them), and rob is the maximum if the current node is robbed (so we must skip both children). The recurrence: skip = max(left[0], left[1]) + max(right[0], right[1]) and rob = node.val + left[0] + right[0]. Finally, the answer is the maximum of the two values returned from the root.

Time Complexity: O(n) — each node is visited exactly once.
Space Complexity: O(h) — recursion stack depth, where h is the height of the tree (worst-case O(n) for skewed tree).

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public int rob(TreeNode root) {
        int[] ans = dfs(root);
        return Math.max(ans[0], ans[1]);
    }
    private int[] dfs(TreeNode node) {
        if(node == null) {
            return new int[]{0,0};
        }
        int[] left = dfs(node.left);
        int[] right = dfs(node.right);
        int skip = Math.max(left[0], left[1]) + Math.max(right[0], right[1]);
        int rob = node.val + left[0] + right[0];
        return new int[]{skip, rob};
    }
}
